program defer_scopes

? A defer that mentions a loop counter or a block-local cannot run at function
? exit: those names no longer exist by then, and the generated C failed with
? "'i' undeclared". Such a statement now runs at the end of the block it was
? written in, which is where its names are still in scope. A defer that only
? mentions function-level names keeps the documented function-exit behaviour
? (see free_defer.ahoy and the defer tests).

@ main ||:
	expected: []

	loop i:0 to 2 do
		defer print|f"iter {i}"|
	$

	if true then
		x: 5
		defer print|f"x={x}"|
	$

	expected.push|"iter 0", "iter 1", "x=5"|
	print|expected|
$
